# GRCh38 fixed learning-curve training subsets

These datasets are generated by `src/make_GRCh38_learning_curves.py` from the
fixed master training pool in `../training/train_100_{pos,neg}.{bed,fa}`.

The fixed master excludes:

- the selected chr1 quota, used as validation; and
- the selected chr2 quota, used as the final test set.

Validation and test files remain in `../training/` and are not copied or
percentage-subsetted here.
Negative membership follows manifest source-pool provenance rather than the genomic
coordinate chosen for the synthetic null sequence by `genNullSeqs()`.

## Nested-subset strategy

Positive and negative BED/FASTA pairs are shuffled independently exactly once
using deterministic random streams derived from seed 42. Every percentage is an
exact prefix of that fixed order:

```text
10% subset of 20% subset of 40% subset of 60% subset of 80% subset of 100%
```

Percentages use `floor(N * percentage / 100)`, with 100% set exactly to `N`.
For the current 167,410-record-per-class master:

| percentage | positive records | negative records |
|-----------:|-----------------:|-----------------:|
| 10%        |           16,741 |           16,741 |
| 20%        |           33,482 |           33,482 |
| 40%        |           66,964 |           66,964 |
| 60%        |          100,446 |          100,446 |
| 80%        |          133,928 |          133,928 |
| 100%       |          167,410 |          167,410 |

## Files

For each percentage `P`, the stage writes:

```text
train_P_pos.bed
train_P_pos.fa
train_P_neg.bed
train_P_neg.fa
```

BED and FASTA records remain synchronized by position. Sequence identifiers and
contents are preserved. `learning_curve_summary.json` records the seed,
rounding rule, record counts, and chromosome counts for every class and
percentage.

The builder validates written counts, unique FASTA identifiers, BED/FASTA record
counts, monotonic sizes, and exact prefix nesting. Each percentage must be used
to train a newly initialized LS-GKM model or AlphaGenome classification head;
larger percentages must not continue training from smaller-percentage models.
